course: dictionary
lesson: 6332
title: Mouldy: two meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Mouldy has two. The first is about food and damp places: «wet hay goes mouldy» means covered with mould.
explain: The second is informal, used in the UK. «A mouldy old jumper» does not mean a jumper covered in fungus. It means an old, mean or nasty jumper. Ask yourself what the sentence is about, then try your meaning in it to see if it still makes sense.
explain: Mould is a soft, furry fungus. It grows best in warm, damp places and breaks down old food. That is why «food goes in the fridge» and why mouldy food should be thrown away.
fact: Mouldy means covered with mould, a soft, furry fungus.
fact: Mould grows best on old food and in warm, damp places.
fact: Mould breaks down old food, so it is a decomposer.
fact: Don't eat mouldy food, and don't sniff it, because the spores can make you ill.
fact: Informal UK use: «a mouldy old jumper» means an old, mean or nasty jumper.
exercise: «Wet hay goes mouldy» in the barn. What does mouldy mean here? | *Covered with mould, a soft, furry fungus | Old and mean | Freshly cut and green | Dried in the sun
exercise: Sam wears «a mouldy old jumper» to school. What does mouldy mean here? | *Old, mean or nasty, an informal word | Wet and cold | Covered with mould
exercise: What should you do with mouldy food? | *Throw it away and don't eat it | Put it back in the fridge | Sniff it to check it is safe | Eat it quickly before it spreads
test: «Damp rooms can get mouldy walls.» What does mouldy mean here? | Clean and freshly painted | Old and mean | Dry and warm | *Covered with mould
test: Germs grow fast in food left warm. Where does mould grow best? | In cold, dry places | *In warm, damp places | In the freezer | Under the hot sun in a desert
exam: Which meaning of mouldy fits «a mouldy old jumper»? | Covered with mud | Wet and cold | Covered with mould | *Old, mean or nasty
